I am not sure you understood what I was saying; it was about accidents
and preventing them, not about intentional container nestings. One way
to prevent accidents is naming the object, or rather *calling* the
object class, so that it is visible in the user interface. Now if you
*name* an individual container to achieve a similar effect you have
_decoupled_ the warning from the dangerous item which is a Bad Thing
if you ask me because it opens room for a couple more mistakes to make.
That's an inherent property of the decoupling. But your level of doing
mistakes might be good enough to avoid that - mine isn't -, so YMMV,
of course.

To answer your question (unrelated to the point I tried to make) how
often I intentionally nest containers. Rarely if I collect spare sacks
for the seven layers of a BoH in BoH setup. But occasionally if I am
close to the next burden level and want to put my "curse absorber sack"
into the BoH to become less burdened. And sometimes I put one container
into another by accident, because I do a lot of actions with tools and
my BoH, because some of the very useful tools are quite heavy.

